        <div id="models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which XTERRA Models This Applies To</h2>
<p>
            XTERRA's published method is written for its treadmills in general, and the steps above
            match the TRX4500 manual we read. The figures that differ between models are the interval
            and, sometimes, the exact distance and quantity, so check the manual for yours.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Compact series (TRX1000, TRX1200, TRX1400) and the older TR150.</strong> These
            folding treadmills use a deck and belt of the kind XTERRA's method describes. Check the
            manual for the interval; if the console has no LUBE reminder, use XTERRA's general rule
            and the every-other-month check. Our guide to <a href="/xterra-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">XTERRA treadmills</a> covers the current range.</li>
            <li><strong>Larger TRX models.</strong> XTERRA gives the TRX3500 an 80 hour interval, and
            the TRX4500 manual we read gives 90 hours, with a console reminder.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking treadmills and desk models.</strong> Low walking machines leave little
            room to reach under the belt, and some walking pads in general use maintenance-free decks.
            Follow the manual for these; if it says not to lubricate, do not. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricating a walking pad</a> covers the low-deck method where lubrication is called for.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Signs Your XTERRA Needs Lubricating</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>The LUBE message</strong> on consoles that have one.</li>
            <li><strong>A dry deck.</strong> The every-other-month finger check finds no slickness.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt hesitation.</strong> The belt seems to catch or slow briefly as your foot
            lands, especially at low speed.</li>
            <li><strong>A harder-working motor.</strong> The motor note rises, or the machine feels as
            if it is dragging, over weeks rather than suddenly.</li>
            <li><strong>Squeaking from under the belt.</strong> A squeak can have other causes, but a
            dry deck is a common one; see our guide to <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill squeaking</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A burning smell, a tripping breaker or an error code is not a lubrication job. Stop using
            the machine and contact XTERRA support.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>After Lubricating: Tension and Tracking</h2>
<p>
            XTERRA's method does not loosen the belt, so tension and tracking should be unchanged.
            Still, watch the belt for the first few minutes of walking. If it drifts to one side, centre
            it with small turns of the rear roller bolts as your manual describes; our guide to <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring a treadmill belt</a> explains the quarter-turn method. If it slips under your foot after lubricating, give the
            lubricant longer to spread before tightening anything, because a freshly lubricated deck
            often settles within a session or two.
          </p>
          <p>
            Write down the date and the console hours. That is the simplest way to stay on XTERRA's
            schedule on a console without a reminder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Lubricating an XTERRA Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Each of these either wastes the job or creates a new problem.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Using half a bottle instead of half a tube</h3>
              <p>XTERRA's instruction to use half the tube per side refers to its own single-application tube. Half of a 4 ounce bottle per side is several applications' worth and will make the belt slip. Follow the bottle maker's dose.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping the clean</h3>
              <p>Lubricant applied over dust and old residue turns into a paste that raises friction. Drag a clean, non-fraying cloth under the belt the full length once or twice first, as XTERRA describes.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Clearing the LUBE message without lubricating</h3>
              <p>The reminder is there because the deck is due. Clearing it to make it go away resets the count, and the next reminder will come long after the deck has dried out.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Using the wrong product</h3>
              <p>WD-40, household silicone sprays with solvents, and petroleum oils can damage a belt and deck. Use XTERRA's lubricant, or 100% silicone treadmill lubricant once you have checked with XTERRA.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Unplug, clean under the belt, lay an S-shaped line down each
            side, and walk it in for five minutes.</strong> That is XTERRA's method, on XTERRA's
            general schedule of every 40 hours or three months unless your manual says otherwise,
            with an every-other-month check for a dry deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use XTERRA's own lubricant under warranty. Out of warranty, a 100% silicone product such as
            Spot On's bottle or the Lube-N-Walk kit does the same job, dosed by the bottle rather than
            by XTERRA's tube. For the general principles, see our guide to <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill belt lubrication</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

